<h2>The gut-sleep connection most advice skips</h2>
<p>
  Here is the chain most sleep content never mentions:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>About 80% of your immune system sits along your gut.</strong> A struggling gut means chronic, low-grade inflammation — and inflammation fragments sleep architecture, stealing deep sleep</li>
  <li><strong>Most of your body's serotonin is made in the gut.</strong> Serotonin is the precursor to melatonin, the hormone that tells your body it is time to sleep</li>
  <li><strong>Blood sugar swings disturb sleep.</strong> A gut that cannot handle meals smoothly produces the kind of overnight swings that pull you out of deep sleep</li>
</ul>
<p>
  That is why the usual advice — more magnesium, better sleep hygiene, a new pillow — produces marginal results.
  It is addressing the surface of a problem whose roots are three floors down.
</p>

<h2>What unrefreshing sleep usually travels with</h2>
<p>
  Unrefreshing sleep rarely shows up alone. Most people who come to EmpowerX3 with this complaint recognize more
  than one of these:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>An afternoon crash that caffeine barely holds off</li>
  <li>Bloating that builds as the day goes on</li>
  <li>Sugar and carb cravings that win more often than they should</li>
  <li>Brain fog and a short fuse that have become normal</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Two or more, and your gut is a strong suspect — because digestion, energy, sleep and focus are downstream of
